---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is taming effectiveness?',
    a: 'Taming effectiveness (TE) starts at 100% when a creature is knocked out and drops every time it eats (and whenever it takes damage while unconscious). It converts directly into bonus levels after the tame: Bonus Levels = floor(Wild Level * (TE / 2)). Kibble preserves ~98–99% TE on a 150, raw meat only ~55%. The taming calculator shows TE and the exact post-tame level for every food.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does starve taming save food or accelerate taming?',
    a: 'No. Starve taming does not save food, does not improve taming effectiveness, and does not alter the total time required. Its primary purpose is risk mitigation: by letting the creature\'s food drop to the required threshold before inserting food, you preserve valuable kibble or raw mutton in case a wild predator attacks the tame or another tribe disrupts the pen.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do mutations work and what is the 20/20 rule?',
    a: 'Every baby gets 3 mutation rolls at 2.5% each (7.31% chance of at least one mutation when both parents\' counters are under 20). A mutation adds +2 wild levels to one random stat (Health, Stamina, Oxygen, Food, Weight, or Melee) and rerolls a color region. Once a parent\'s combined counter hits 20, that parent stops producing new mutations—but its stats still inherit normally at 55% odds. By breeding a mutated sire to unmutated 0/20 females, you can stack mutations indefinitely.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many narcotics does a tame need?',
    a: 'Exactly enough to replace the torpor lost during the tame: Narcotic Deficit = (Torpor Drain/s * Tame Duration) - Total Max Torpor. Big-food creatures often need zero (they stay KO\'d naturally); Giganotosaurus at 150 needs 500+ because its torpor drains over 400/s. The taming calculator computes exact narcotic, biotoxin, and berry counts.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which kibble does my creature eat?',
    a: 'Every creature prefers one of six kibble tiers matching its egg-size class, and also accepts any tier above at full 400 affinity. Any lower tier drops affinity to 16 per piece—worse than raw standard meat. Check the reverse-lookup table on the kibble chart page.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the highest level a tame can reach?',
    a: 'On official settings wild creatures max at 150; a perfect 100%-TE tame adds floor(150 / 2) = 75 bonus levels -> 225 right after taming. With mutations the wild level keeps growing (+2 per mutation) up to the server level cap (450 on official ASE for most creatures; 500 for Tek variants).',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the 254 wild stat point cap?',
    a: 'ARK stores stat points as an 8-bit unsigned integer (maximum value 255). If breeding mutations push a creature to exactly 255 wild points in a stat, the game engine disables domestic leveling for that stat! Always stop breeding at 254 points or lower if you plan to train domestic levels.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does imprinting affect speed?',
    a: 'Yes on ground creatures (+20% at 100% imprint in ASE), but no on flyers. Stamina, Oxygen, and Crafting never receive imprint boosts. The 30% damage increase and 30% damage reduction rider bonus applies ONLY while the designated imprinter is riding the creature.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why are narcoberries sometimes better than narcotics?',
    a: 'Narcotics add 40 torpor over 8 seconds; narcoberries add 7.5 torpor over 5 seconds. Because torpor cannot naturally drain while narcotic-induced torpor is actively climbing, feeding stacks of narcoberries keeps torpor frozen for much longer total time, giving you a wider safety window on rapid-drain tames.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How accurate is ARKTools?',
    a: 'Every number is computed from the game\'s own formulas using species constants extracted from the game files (published by the open-source ARK Smart Breeding project) and cross-checked against the ARK Community Wiki. The math is covered by unit tests with hand-worked examples—e.g. the level 150 Rex + 17 Exceptional Kibble -> level 224 case.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do the calculators work for ARK: Survival Ascended?',
    a: 'The formulas and creature constants are from Survival Evolved (v313.5+ rules). ASA uses the same core systems (affinity equations, 55/45 breeding inheritance, mutation roll logic) with minor rebalances and adds Augmented Kibble; treat ASA numbers as very close approximations.',
  },
];

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="ARK Calculators FAQ - Taming, Breeding & Kibble Questions"
  description="Straight answers on ARK: Survival Evolved & Ascended math: what taming effectiveness does, how mutations and the 20/20 rule work, narcotics math, kibble tiers, imprinting and how ARKTools computes every number."
  path="/faq/"
  schema={[schema]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow"><Icon name="circle-help" size={15} /> Field Manual</span>
    <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The critical ARK mechanics questions that decide whether your tame or breeding line is worth it—answered with the actual formulas, not folk wisdom.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    {faqs.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary>{f.q}</summary>
        <div class="faq-body"><p>{f.a}</p></div>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="panel" style="margin-top:2rem">
    <h2 class="with-icon"><Icon name="map" size={20} /> Deep Mechanics References</h2>
    <p>
      Each tool page provides dedicated guides with full mathematical breakdowns:
      <a href="/taming-calculator/">taming mechanics</a>,
      <a href="/breeding-calculator/">inheritance &amp; mutations</a>,
      <a href="/kibble-chart/">the kibble system</a>, and
      <a href="/stat-calculator/">stat layers</a>. For raw sources see
      <a href="/sources/">Data &amp; Sources</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
